-- title:
--   Invariant element avoiding valuation rings over the other supersingular places
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q = 3$, $M'$ is a non-zero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell_g$ is a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$. Write $\bar{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. The level group is $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$, required by `hH₁` to be the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) — the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$ — with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$.
--
--   **Reduction of constants and supersingular places.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ which, by `hA`, lies over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. $W$ is a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` $=$ the subfield of $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A((t))$ generated over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ by the $q$-expansions $j$ and $j(\mathsf q^{M'})$, and `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places: those $w$ that are rational (the structure map to the residue field of $w$ is onto), that are affine geometric places, and whose value $w(j)$ on the generator `jGeomGen` is a supersingular $j$-invariant, i.e. lies in `ssJSet q (ResidueField A)` = the set of $j$ such that every elliptic curve with that invariant has no non-zero $q$-torsion point. `hle` asserts $\overline{F}(M') :=$ `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, the base change to $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ of the level-$M'$ modular function field, is contained in `fieldBar q M'`, the base change of the $X_{H}$-function field at level $q^2M'$ for `levelH q M'`. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ on $\overline F(M')$ with values in `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` (a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, a surjective residue map with kernel the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, compatible with constants and with orders), and `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose image in $\bar{\mathbb Q}((t))$ lies in $\overline F(M')$, that image lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s \in W$ is a distinguished supersingular place.
--
--   **The base local ring.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$, $\pi_0 \in k_0$ with $\pi_0 \in A$ (`hπ`), and $\mathcal O := A \cap k_0$ denotes `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres`, `hκ` state that $\mathcal O$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, is Henselian, has algebraically closed residue field, and that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary constants.** A prime $\ell$ with $3 \le \ell$, $\ell \ne q$, $\ell \nmid M'$ (`hℓ`, `hℓ3`, `hℓq`, `hℓM'`); $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity in $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ (`hζ₀`); $\varpi_t \in k_0$ with $\varpi_t \in A$ and $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$ (`hϖtA`, `hϖt`), together with $\varpi_t' \in \mathcal O$ equal to $\varpi_t$ (`hϖt'`); an intermediate field $K_b$ of $\bar{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ required to be trivial ($K_b = \bot$, `hKb`) carrying a valuation subring $A_b$ whose members are exactly the elements lying in $A$ (`hAb`), with maximal ideal generated by a non-zero $\varpi_b$ (`hϖb`, `hϖb0`); $\xi \in k_0$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hξ`); and `htame`, requiring $\ell_g - 1$ to be a unit of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The function field and its $j$-chart.** $K_\ell$ is an intermediate field of $k_0((t))/k_0$, required by `hKℓ` to be `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`, that is, the subfield generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise images of the $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$-function field; $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ is non-zero and equals the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` of $j$ (`hjℓ`); $K_\ell$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra compatibly with $k_0$. Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin 𝒪 K_ℓ j_ℓ`, the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $\mathcal O[j_\ell]$, and `jChartFin` for $j_\ell$ viewed in $C$. An automorphism $\tau \in \operatorname{Aut}_{k_0}(K_\ell)$ is called a *level automorphism at* $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ when [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ Kℓ τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) holds: for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f,g$ on $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ of weight $k$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$, $p_g \neq 0$, every $x \in K_\ell$ whose Laurent series is the image of $p_f/p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, one has $\iota(\tau x)\cdot \mathrm{qExp}(g\mid_k \gamma^{(q)}) = \mathrm{qExp}(f\mid_k \gamma^{(q)})$, where $\gamma^{(q)} = \begin{pmatrix} a & b/q\\ qc & d\end{pmatrix}$ for $\gamma = \begin{pmatrix} a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$.
--
--   **The point $y$.** $y$ is an ideal of $C$, maximal (`hy`) and containing $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`). `hss` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the image $\varphi(j_\ell)$ is a supersingular $j$-invariant in the above sense. `hover` states that $y$ lies over $s$: for every $g$ in the level-$M'$ modular function field `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` whose image lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, which is regular wherever $j$ is (at every place $P$ of $\overline F(M')$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(j)$ implies $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(g)$), and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose reduction in $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ equals the value at $s$ of that residue, the element of $C$ given by the image of $g$ satisfies: that element minus the image of $c$ lies in $y$. `hOrbit` states that any maximal ideal $y'$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same lying-over-$s$ condition is in the orbit of $y$: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, with $f \in y \iff \tau f \in y'$ for $f \in C$.
--
--   **The integral model block.** $B_t$ is an $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$ and $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` (fourteen clauses, summarised here) requires: $C \subseteq B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $\mathcal O$; $B_t/(\pi_0)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t \cap k_0 = \mathcal O$; the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by $\pi_0$ and $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring; $y$ is the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_t$ to $C$, and $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ away from that maximal ideal; for every $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa$ of $\mathcal O$ there is a surjection $\rho : B_t \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q κ`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel the elements lying in the maximal ideal of $W_t$, compatible with constants, and such that every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ with a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ is matched by a scalar $c \in \mathrm{GF}(q^2)^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c) \in$ [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting through [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325), with $c \ne 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$, and with the same $c$ realised by some $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ paired with the identity matrix; $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is carried onto the one cut out by $W_t$ by some level automorphism; and an element of $B_t$ all of whose $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$-translates lie in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   **Inertia, ends and crossing data.** Further data: $n \ge 1$ with $n \mid q+1$ (`hn1`, `hnq`); $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ (`hγ₀Γ`, `hγ₀Γ0`) and $\tau_0$ a level automorphism at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ (`hτ₀`) with $f \in W_t \iff \tau_0 f \in W_t$ (`hτ₀W`); $m \ge 1$ with $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' \cdot(\text{unit})$ in $\mathcal O$ (`hm1`, `hm`); $\zeta_c$ in the adic completion $\widehat{\mathcal O}$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$ (`hζc`, `hζcprim`); the $q$-expansion $j(\mathsf q^{\,q})$ $=$ `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` lies in $K_\ell$ (`hjK`) and in $C$ (`hjC`), and $a_0 \in \mathcal O$ is such that $j(\mathsf q^{\,q}) - a_0 \in y$ (`ha₀y`); a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` (twelve clauses, summarised here) requires: $\tau_0^{\,n}$ is the identity on $B_t$ and no smaller power is; every level automorphism attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with a power $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; `ends` has $q+1$ elements, is permuted by the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$ that preserve $W_t$, transitively so, each end being preserved by $\tau_0$, distinct ends being separated by an element which is a non-unit in one and a unit in the other; a finite-type, level-stable subalgebra $B_c$ contained in every end and generating each end by localisation; a criterion deducing preservation of $W_t$ from mapping an end into $W_t$; and, for every end $O$: $O \subseteq W_t$, $O$ is local and Noetherian, contains $C$, satisfies $O \cap k_0 = \mathcal O$, every element of $O$ is congruent to a constant, and the adic completion of $O$ is isomorphic, by an isomorphism $\iota$ fixing constants, to the crossing model $\widehat{\mathcal O}[[U,V]]/(UV - \pi_0^m)$ in such a way that distinguished elements $c_x, c_y \in O$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m\cdot(\text{unit})$ go to unit multiples of $U$ and of $V$, with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, that $j(\mathsf q^{\,q}) - a_0$ goes to a unit multiple of $V^e$ with $e \ge 1$, and that $\tau_0$ is conjugated into an $\widehat{\mathcal O}$-automorphism $\theta_0$ multiplying $U$ by $\zeta_c$ and $V$ by $\zeta_c^{-1}$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal; together with a valuation subring $W_x$ of $K_\ell$ and a subalgebra $B_x$ with the listed integrality, stability, centring and separation properties relative to $O$, $y$ and the other ends; and finally a dichotomy: a valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ with $V \cap k_0 = \mathcal O$ containing $C$ and with $y$ inside its maximal ideal either contains $B_t$ or contains an end $O$ whose non-units lie in the maximal ideal of $V$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hR9` provides an element $b \in B_t$ lying in no valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ with $V \cap k_0 = \mathcal O$, with $\pi_0$ in the maximal ideal of $V$, containing $j_\ell$, and at which no value at $j_\ell$ of a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ lies in the maximal ideal of $V$. The hypothesis `hR10` provides, for every maximal ideal $y''$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ which is *not* in the $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$-orbit of $y$ (no level automorphism matches $y$ with $y''$ on $C$), an element $b \in B_t$ which, for every $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$, lies in no valuation subring of $K_\ell$ containing $C$ and centred at the $\tau$-translate of $y''$. The hypothesis `hn` fixes $n = 2\cdot$`placeWidthChar q M' s`, where for $q = 3$ the width character is $6$ or $1$ according as the $j$-value of the place is $0$ or not, divided by the ramification index of $j$ at the place.
--
--   **Naming block.** $G$ is the subgroup of $\operatorname{Aut}_{k_0}(K_\ell)$ generated by all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ (`hG`); $K_0$ is the fixed field of $G$ (`hK₀`); $B_0$ is the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$ consisting of the $G$-invariant elements of $B_t$ (`hB₀`); and $W_0'$ is the valuation subring of $K_0$ whose members are the elements lying in $W_t$ (`hW₀'`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $s' \in W$ with $s' \ne s$ there exists $b \in K_0$ such that $b$, viewed in $K_\ell$, lies in $B_0$, and such that $b \notin V$ for every valuation subring $V$ of $K_0$ satisfying the following two conditions.
--
--   First, $V$ induces $A$ on $k_0$: for every $x \in k_0$, $x$ lies in $A$ if and only if the image of $x$ in $K_0$ lies in $V$.
--
--   Second, $V$ is centred at $s'$ in the following sense: for every $g$ in `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` whose image in $\overline F(M')$ lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, such that $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(g)$ at every place $P$ of $\overline F(M')$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ at which $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(j)$, and such that the $R_0$-residue of $g$ lies in the valuation subring of $s'$, and for every witness that the coefficientwise image $\mathrm{coeffEmb}_{k_0}(g)$ lies in $K_\ell$: that element lies in $K_0$, its class in $K_0$ lies in $V$, and for every $c \in k_0$ with $c \in A$ whose reduction in $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ equals the value at $s'$ of the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the difference between that class and the image of $c$ in $K_0$ lies in $V$ and belongs to the maximal ideal of $V$.
--
--   This is the separation step in the descent of the auxiliary full-level integral model to the fixed field $K_0$ of the level group: it supplies, for each supersingular place other than the chosen one $s$, a $G$-invariant element of the integral model which lies in no valuation ring of $K_0$ centred at that other place, so that the local ring to be built at $s$ is not contaminated by the remaining supersingular points. It is used in the construction of the semistable chart data (supersingular discrete valuation ring, affine chart, poles, moduli Hasse relation, common chart, nodes and the Igusa/deck separation clauses) in the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ diamond frame at $q = 3$.
